#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bessel_k

#% text
# `bessel_k(nu, x)` gives the modified Bessel function of the second kind `K_nu(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `K_nu(x)` solves the modified Bessel equation and decays like `sqrt(pi/(2*x))*exp(-x)` (DLMF 10.25.3); it is defined for `x > 0` and singular at 0.

#% item
# Half-integer orders give closed forms; machine numbers and balls give numerical values. `K_nu` equals `K_(-nu)`.

#% item
# `bessel_k` is listable in both arguments.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
bessel_k(0, 1.0)
#% output
#= 0.42102443824070834

#% cell
bessel_k(1, 2.5)
#% output
#= 0.073890816347747

#% cell
bessel_k(1/2, x)
#% output
#= 2^(1/2)*exp(-x)*pi^(1/2)/(2*x^(1/2))

#% cell
n(bessel_k(0, 1), 30)
#% output
#= 0.4210244382407083333356273792126090

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Large arguments decay exponentially:

#% cell
bessel_k(0, 100.0)
#% output
#= 4.656628229175903e-45

#% text
# A fractional order:

#% cell
bessel_k(1/3, 2.0)
#% output
#= 0.11654496129616529

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# An integral representation, `K_0(x)` as the integral of `exp(-x*cosh(t))`:

#% cell
n_integrate(exp(-cosh(t)), [t, 0, inf]) - bessel_k(0, 1.0)
#% output
#= 2.220446049250313e-16

#% text
# The Wronskian `I_0 K_1 + I_1 K_0 = 1/x`:

#% cell
bessel_i(0, 2.0)*bessel_k(1, 2.0) + bessel_i(1, 2.0)*bessel_k(0, 2.0)
#% output
#= 0.5000000000000004

